# os.capabilities — the OS-native layer's capability declaration (Core v5, #663/#664).
#
# COPY THIS to your OS repo as os/<os>.capabilities (beside os/<os>.zsh,
# os/<os>.conf and os/<os>.gitconfig) and fill in the archive's real verbs.
# bootstrap.sh links it to $ZDOTDIR/os.capabilities via blib_link_os_layer; Core's
# zsh/02-capabilities.zsh READS it (it is never sourced) into $_CORE_CAP, and
# maint/dotfiles-maint.sh — which is bash, not zsh — reads the same file with sed.
#
# The values below are Fedora's, transcribed from PORTING-MATRIX.md §"Package-manager
# commands". They are an EXAMPLE, not a default: nothing in Core falls back to them, and
# since #763 Core carries no per-manager defaults of its own either. A box with no linked
# declaration has no upgrade verb, no scheduler unit directory and no install hint — each
# consumer says so in its own voice. THE DECLARATION IS THE WHOLE SOURCE OF TRUTH.
#
# Validate a real one with:  core/scripts/check-capabilities.sh os/<os>.capabilities
# That script is where the schema is defined, and it is what rejects an unknown key.
#
# FORMAT. One KEY=value per line. Values are command PREFIXES and may contain spaces;
# they are not quoted, not expanded and never eval'd as arbitrary strings. Everything
# after the first `=` is the value, verbatim, minus trailing whitespace. Lines that are
# blank or start with `#` are ignored. Keys are [A-Z][A-Z0-9_]*.
#
# A `#` INSIDE A VALUE IS NOT A COMMENT. The reader keeps it, so
# `PKG_OWNS=dnf provides   # which package owns this` declares a verb that does not
# exist. Put the note on its own line above. The validator rejects this.

# ── package-manager verbs (all required) ──────────────────────────────────────
PKG_REFRESH=sudo dnf check-update
# THE INTERACTIVE upgrade verb — no `-y` here. `up` without a flag must still let the
# manager print its transaction summary and ask; auto-confirm is PKG_ASSUME_YES, below.
PKG_UPGRADE=sudo dnf upgrade --refresh
PKG_INSTALL=sudo dnf install -y
PKG_REMOVE=sudo dnf remove -y
PKG_SEARCH=dnf search
PKG_OWNS=dnf provides
# What `up`'s once-a-day "N updates available" nudge counts. Kept separate from
# PKG_REFRESH because it is the single most divergent verb across the seven archives —
# and the one Core hardcoded worst (`grep -qi tumbleweed /etc/os-release` to choose
# between `zypper dup` and `zypper up` lived in zsh/60-update.zsh).
PKG_COUNT_PENDING=dnf -q --refresh check-update

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# systemd | launchd | cron | none. `none` is a real answer — a container, or a box with
# neither — and tells the maint layer to offer the manual verb rather than claim a
# timer it cannot install. `cron` is what an OpenRC box (Alpine, Gentoo) gets: it has
# crontab and no systemd.
SCHEDULER=systemd
# The DIRECTORY this box's scheduler reads units from. REQUIRED for systemd and launchd;
# cron takes none (its entry lives in the user's crontab, not a file of its own) and
# neither does `none`.
#
# A DIRECTORY, not a full path — Core appends its own unit name. WHERE units live is an
# OS fact and belongs to you; what Core calls its job (dotfiles-maint.service,
# com.dotfiles.maint) is Core's identity and appears in `systemctl enable` and `launchctl`.
#
# THIS IS THE KEY THAT GETS THE LAST OS-ABSOLUTE PATH OUT OF CORE. An absolute path is
# CORRECT here and wrong in Core — the layering rule in one line. macOS declares
# ~/Library/LaunchAgents.
SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR=~/.config/systemd/user

# ── how `up` applies (all optional, #664) ─────────────────────────────────────
# The flag `up -y` appends. OMIT IT to mean "never auto-confirm" — the right answer on
# Arch (partial-upgrade breakage), Gentoo (`-a` asks regardless) and Alpine.
PKG_ASSUME_YES=-y
# Upgrade only the named packages. OMITTING THIS IS A SAFETY DECLARATION: `up -i` refuses
# on an archive that declares no partial verb, which is how a rolling distro says "this
# must update as a whole". Do not add one to be helpful.
PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL=sudo dnf upgrade --refresh
# PKG_UPGRADE_PRE — run before the upgrade, on both the full and partial paths; a failure
# ABORTS. dnf refreshes in one verb, so Fedora needs none. Debian: sudo apt-get update.
# PKG_CLEANUP — run after a successful FULL upgrade. Debian: sudo apt-get autoremove.

# ── how `up` counts (all optional, #664) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Core runs PKG_COUNT_PENDING and takes ONE package name per matching line: keep the
# lines matching PKG_PENDING_MATCH (default `.`), print field PKG_PENDING_FIELD
# (default 1) split on PKG_PENDING_FS (default whitespace). Passed to awk as DATA — a
# declaration is never eval'd. dnf's output needs only the match:
#
# AN ERE CANNOT END IN A LITERAL SPACE. The reader trims trailing whitespace, so write
# `^Inst[[:space:]]`, never `^Inst ` — the latter is silently stored as `^Inst` and then
# matches a line the archive never meant it to.
PKG_PENDING_MATCH=^[a-zA-Z0-9][^ ]*[[:space:]]
# PKG_COUNT_REFRESH — run before PKG_COUNT_PENDING in the COUNT path only, never the
# list path. Homebrew's outdated set is stale without it; no other archive needs one.
#
# PKG_COUNT_EXIT_TRUSTED=1 — a non-zero exit from PKG_COUNT_PENDING means "could not
# answer", so the count reports the -1 unknown sentinel instead of 0. OFF by default,
# because most archives overload that status: dnf exits 100 when updates EXIST, and
# `pacman -Qu` / `checkupdates` exit non-zero when there are NONE.

# ── the scheduled runner (optional, #665) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Set to 1 to permit the daily maint run to apply SYSTEM package upgrades on this box.
# OMITTING IT REFUSES, and that direction is the point: a fail-open here silently enables
# unattended full upgrades on a box nobody is watching. It is the SECOND of two gates —
# the operator's MAINT_SYSTEM_UPGRADE=1 is the first, and both must be true.
#
# WHO SHOULD NOT DECLARE IT: Kali (engagement boxes are updated by hand between ops, so a
# background upgrade breaks reproducibility mid-engagement), and Arch and Gentoo (a rolling
# distro that half-upgrades unattended is how you get a box that will not boot).
MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE=1

# ── optional ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Space-separated tools core-doctor reports as OPT-IN rather than MISSING on this OS:
# things the archive has but this repo deliberately does not install. Omit the key
# entirely to accept Core's built-in default (_CORE_DOCTOR_OPTIN, zsh/30-functions.zsh).
#
# A DECLARED LIST IS COMPLETE FOR THIS BOX — it REPLACES Core's default, it does not add to
# it. So a repo that declares this key must re-state every tool it still considers optional,
# not only the ones it is adding: declaring `TOOLS_OPTIN=jj` alone would make lnav,
# hyperfine and the rest of Core's default list report as MISSING here. Start from the
# default above and edit it.
#
# THIS KEY IS THE ONE PLACE A PARTIAL DECLARATION FALLS BACK per-key (#666). Everywhere
# else — PKG_ASSUME_YES, PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL, MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE — an omission is a
# SAFETY statement and Core honours it exactly. Omitting this one says only that you have
# not curated a list, so Core's default applies rather than "nothing here is optional",
# which would mark every uninstalled optional tool as degraded.
TOOLS_OPTIN=lnav hyperfine watchexec shellcheck shfmt ouch git-absorb jnv gping

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE OTHER ARCHIVES. These were Core's built-in rows in zsh/60-update.zsh, which is what
# every box ran before #667 transcribed them into the seven OS repos and #763 deleted the
# table. They are kept here as the reference a NEW OS repo starts from — a declaration that
# differs from its row is a deliberate change, not a transcription slip. The live values are
# each repo's own os/<os>.capabilities; PORTING-MATRIX.md's package-manager table is
# generated from those and is the place to read what the fleet actually declares.
#
#   Debian/Ubuntu/Kali (apt)
#     PKG_UPGRADE=sudo apt-get full-upgrade
#     PKG_UPGRADE_PRE=sudo apt-get update
#     PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL=sudo apt-get install --only-upgrade
#     PKG_CLEANUP=sudo apt-get autoremove
#     PKG_ASSUME_YES=-y
#     PKG_COUNT_PENDING=apt-get -s upgrade
#     PKG_PENDING_MATCH=^Inst[[:space:]]
#     PKG_PENDING_FIELD=2
#
#   openSUSE (zypper) — Tumbleweed is `dup`, Leap is `up`, and ONE repo serves both.
#   Core's built-in row probed /etc/os-release for this, which is exactly the OS knowledge
#   Core may not carry; a declaration cannot probe, so decide it deliberately. The repo
#   ships TWO declarations (opensuse + opensuse.leap), which is the shape to copy.
#     PKG_UPGRADE=sudo zypper dup          # Tumbleweed;  Leap: sudo zypper up
#     PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL=sudo zypper up
#     PKG_ASSUME_YES=-y
#     PKG_COUNT_PENDING=zypper -q list-updates
#     PKG_PENDING_MATCH=^v[[:space:]]
#     PKG_PENDING_FS=|
#     PKG_PENDING_FIELD=3
#
#   macOS (Homebrew) — never privileged, so no sudo prefix anywhere.
#     PKG_UPGRADE=brew upgrade
#     PKG_UPGRADE_PRE=brew update
#     PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL=brew upgrade
#     PKG_CLEANUP=brew cleanup
#     PKG_COUNT_PENDING=brew outdated --quiet
#     PKG_COUNT_REFRESH=brew update
#     SCHEDULER=launchd
#     SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR=~/Library/LaunchAgents
#
#   Arch (pacman) — no PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL and no PKG_ASSUME_YES, deliberately.
#     PKG_UPGRADE=sudo pacman -Syu
#     PKG_COUNT_PENDING=checkupdates       # pacman-contrib; syncs a copy in USER space
#
#   Alpine (apk) — doas, not sudo. Full sync only, like Arch. OpenRC, so SCHEDULER=cron
#   (busybox crond) and no SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR.
#     PKG_UPGRADE=doas apk upgrade
#     PKG_UPGRADE_PRE=doas apk update
#     PKG_COUNT_PENDING=apk list -u
#
#   Gentoo (emerge) — `-a` always asks, so PKG_ASSUME_YES would be a lie.
#     PKG_UPGRADE=sudo emerge -auvDN @world
#     PKG_UPGRADE_PRE=sudo emerge --sync
#     PKG_COUNT_PENDING=<a script this repo ships>
#     PKG_COUNT_EXIT_TRUSTED=1
#   Counting on Gentoo needs more than a command line: `eix -u` answers "is a newer
#   version in the tree?" and `up` runs `emerge -uDN @world`, which answers "what will
#   actually change?" — permanently different questions, off by 70-vs-8 on a real box
#   (#753/#756). Only the resolver knows, and its output needs parsing, so declare a
#   script the repo ships rather than a one-liner. Core's built-in row named a function of
#   its own for this; that function went with the table in #763, and a declaration must not
#   reach into Core's internals in any case. dotfiles-Gentoo ships scripts/pkg-pending.sh
#   and declares it as `gentoo-pkg-pending` — copy that shape.
#   PKG_COUNT_EXIT_TRUSTED says a non-zero exit means "could not answer": an
#   `emerge --pretend` that cannot resolve must report unknown, never 0.
